# Supabase schema changes: committed migrations only

The shared Supabase data plane's schema is git-owned. The ONLY source of
schema changes is committed files in `edviro-dashboard/supabase/migrations/`,
applied by CI:

- Staging branch DB — applies automatically on push to edviro-dashboard's
  `staging` branch (Supabase GitHub App).
- Production — applies only via the dispatch-only `db-push-prod` workflow,
  after the code that depends on the migration is deployed.
- Local / CI e2e databases — `supabase db reset --local` (exempt from these
  rules).

FORBIDDEN against production or the staging branch, from ANY repo:

- Supabase MCP `apply_migration` — never sanctioned, against any target
- DDL through MCP `execute_sql` (create / alter / drop / grant / revoke /
  truncate / comment)
- `supabase db push`, `supabase migration repair`, psql DDL, or hand-edits
  to `supabase_migrations.schema_migrations`

Invariants:

1. Commit before apply — a migration file must be committed on
   edviro-dashboard `staging` before any shared database records its
   version. An applied-but-uncommitted migration bricks every other
   agent's staging pushes (db-drift-check: "applied versions with NO
   matching file").
2. Versions applied to production are permanent — never rename those
   files. Only prod-pending files may be restamped; afterwards recreate
   the staging branch (`edviro-dashboard/scripts/staging.sh down && up`).
3. MCP `execute_sql` is for reads; data seeding/QA writes are allowed on
   the staging branch only.

Need a schema change while working in another repo? Write the migration
file in edviro-dashboard, commit it there, let CI apply it — then continue
your work against the applied schema.

History: 2026-08-27 (laptop `db push`) and 2026-08-29 (MCP `apply_migration`
of an uncommitted file) each bricked all staging deploys for every agent. A
user-level Cursor hook (`~/.cursor/hooks/guard-supabase-ddl.py`) now denies
these calls at the tool level — if it blocks you, fix your approach, don't
work around it.
